uct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-backlog-roi-2026-11-18.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/blind-verification-join.md", ".prawduct/changes/branch-review-set.md", ".prawduct/changes/chunk-credit-merged-verified.md", ".prawduct/changes/closes-cache-miss.md", ".prawduct/changes/control-check-field.md", ".prawduct/changes/detached-refresh-outcome.md", ".prawduct/changes/exit1-covering-mode.md", ".prawduct/changes/gate-review-concurrency.md", ".prawduct/changes/gate-span-and-coverage-slips.md", ".prawduct/changes/late-requirement-capture.md", ".prawduct/changes/measure-record-findings.md", ".prawduct/changes/pushed-branch-never-prd.md", ".prawduct/changes/records-pass-final-bars.md", ".prawduct/changes/round-tally-at-next-action.md", ".prawduct/changes/stop-span-bash-authorship.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md", ".prawduct/project-state.yaml", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/README.md", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/join.py"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "blocking", "title": "Two tests fail in the saved evidence for the reviewed tree", "recommendation": "Instance. `prawduct-hook test-status --wait` reports 2 failures in evidence recorded against evidence_tree facbf1f6, which is this review's head_tree. (1) `test_next_action_warns_about_the_supersession_deadline` still asserts the 'Act now ... only a spanning cumulative' sentence that Chunk 04 removed from `_next_action_core`. (2) `test_no_shipped_file_cites_a_NORM_from_an_artifact_the_reader_does_not_have` catches `plugin/lib/control_checks.py:5`, which cites ``.prawduct/artifacts/nonfunctional-requirements.md`` \u00a7 Direction from a shipped file. Commit f3fb3e9d, made after this dispatch, edits exactly these two sites, but that tree is outside this review. Record a suite on it and clear this finding through verify-resolutions.", "files": ["plugin/lib/control_checks.py", "tests/test_carried_blocking_findings.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "failed_tests": ["tests.test_carried_blocking_findings::test_next_action_warns_about_the_supersession_deadline", "tests.test_plugin_packaging::test_no_shipped_file_cites_a_NORM_from_an_artifact_the_reader_does_not_have"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "blocking", "title": "The branch review set's worktree arm is unbounded, so on an empty span every new consumer sweeps in every review this worktree ever recorded", "recommendation": "Class. Why it broke: `coverage.branch_review_ids` falls back to `_reviews_recorded_in_this_worktree` whenever merge_base..HEAD has no commits. That set has no scope, time or anchor bound. Before #334 its only consumer was the round budget, which narrows it by scope. Chunk 04 gave it unnarrowed consumers: `carried_blocking` at the dispatch roll-call, the consolidation NOT DONE line, the derived cache and begin_review's `actionable` count; bare `render-dispositions`; `gates._roll_call_review_ids`; and `briefing._branch_findings_line`. The empty span is a normal state: a branch cut and not yet committed to (a dirty-tree chunk review, then verify-resolutions), a trunk repo after push, or the base branch checked out at its remote. In that state a verify pass is handed a roll-call of every unresolved blocker any earlier, unrelated review recorded from this checkout. Measured read-only on this clone's store, the primary checkout's worktree holds 1196 review facts, 21 of them with 24 unresolved blocking findings. Consolidation then reports NOT DONE for findings the pass never saw, begin_review can never return 'nothing to verify', and the session handoff reports those counts as 'this branch's'. No test covers this arm for any consumer except the budget (`test_the_budget_narrows_the_branch_set_by_scope_on_both_bounds`). The tests seed one review per worktree. Fix it at the owner, not per consumer: bound the worktree arm by an anchor (for example, reviews whose `head_commit`/`dispatch_commit` equals the merge-base/HEAD, the only commit an empty span can have reviews on), or return a distinct status that non-budget consumers read as 'anchor alone'. Add a test that seeds an older, other-scope review with an unresolved blocker in the same worktree and asserts it is not carried, rendered or counted.", "files": ["plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/dispositions.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/briefing.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "blocking", "title": "Two answers to 'is this blocker open': the branch review set (#334) counts a blocker the gate already treats as cleared by a spanning review", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: #334 added a second predicate for 'open blocker' (no resolution fact, over coverage.branch_review_ids) beside the gate's own (coverage_algebra._unresolved_at_nodes, which lists only blockers at trees on the composed path), and nothing reconciles them. The gate clears a blocker in two ways: a resolution fact, or a spanning review that never stands at that tree. Its own docstring says so ('Clearing it takes a resolution fact ... or a spanning review that never stands at the tree in question'), as do review-cycle.md and test_cumulative_gate's 'self-heals through a spanning review'. The branch set honours only the first. Reachable state: round 1 records blocker B at tree T1. B is cleared the way the gate's pre-#334 remedy prescribed, by a clean spanning `cumulative` (or a later `final`/`cumulative` path that routes around T1). The gate now reports covered, while (a) every later verify-resolutions dispatch carries B in its roll-call (carried_blocking), (b) begin_review counts B as actionable, so a pass the gate does not need is no longer refused as 'no-review-needed', (c) consolidation persists a NOT DONE next_action into .critic-findings.json saying 'the gate reads facts, so it still blocks', which is false, and (d) every session handoff's 'Branch findings' line (briefing._branch_findings_line, via dispositions.census open_blocking, whose comment claims 'the gate's terms') reports B as open blocking. Branches that followed the old 'Superseded: run a spanning cumulative' advice are exactly the ones that now hold these phantom blockers. The builder sees two computed surfaces disagree, and the cheapest way to silence the briefing and NOT DONE is a verify round the gate never asked for. Fix through one owner: derive 'still open' for the branch set from the same composition the gate uses. One way is to drop from carried_blocking, and from the census's open_blocking, any blocker whose review's head_tree is not a node on the gate's covering path (coverage_algebra already exposes path nodes). Another is to make a covering spanning review count as clearing in coverage_algebra.resolution-side terms, which every surface already reads. Then add a test where a clean merge-base\u2192HEAD review follows a blocked round, and assert that carried_blocking, the census, the briefing line and check-cumulative-critic all agree.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/briefing.py", "plugin/lib/dispositions.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The review-chain link (base_tree = head_tree of the newest earlier review) is now walked in three hand-copied places", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: plan_status._extended_reviews (new, #1191) re-implements the link that critic_mode.review_chain owns, and its docstring cites that owner ('The link is critic_mode.review_chain's') instead of calling it. critic_consolidate.carried_blocking's anchor loop is a third copy of the same link. The three already differ in small ways: identity by id() vs by fact id, all reviews at a linked tree vs only the newest, and exclusion of the current review. The next change to the link (bounding it to a worktree or lineage, say) will reach one copy, and chunk credit, the verify chain and the roll-call anchor will then disagree about which review a verify pass extends. Extract one function that returns the prior review a fact links to, e.g. in evidence or critic_mode, imported lazily where layering requires it. Route review_chain, _extended_reviews and carried_blocking's anchor through it. To find any other copies, grep for `head_tree\") == ` / `.get(\"head_tree\") == base` over plugin/lib.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The #1201 Records Pass remedy cannot move the share that triggered it, and the boundary breach has no recorded disposition", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: the remedy was chosen without checking which stage `final` is. `final` is an inner-stage mode (`critic_consolidate.stage_of`; review-cycle.md 'Severity is stage-keyed'; tools/measure-record-findings.py also counts it as `inner`). At the inner stage every verdict outside the inner BLOCKING set is already an observation. So the 'it misleads into action' bar, which rates WARNING, never produced a finding at `final`, and dropping it at `final` (cross-checks.md: 'three bars (`final` skips the second, #771)') changes no finding and buys back no round. The inner-stage share that tripped #771's rule (12% against 5%) has to come from elsewhere: BLOCKING bars, record-lint, or chunk and verify modes, none of which this edit touches. The boundary share also breached (14% against 10%), but `cumulative` keeps all three bars and no recorded reason says why its breach goes unremedied. #771's own rule is that a breach files the next remedy, not that it is waived. The change record carries the false claim outward: 'A `final` review raises fewer WARNINGs on governance records' (records-pass-final-bars.md, Consumer note). Before this change a `final` raised none on that bar, so the sentence clears the Records Pass 'it ships' bar. Fix: re-derive the remedy for the stage that breached. For the inner stage, find out which producer the round-buying record-only findings came from, by mode and by severity, with the measure tool. For the boundary, apply #771's ordered remedy or record why not. Then correct or delete the fragment's consumer-note claim. Do not keep a rule edit whose only effect is to the prose.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/cross-checks.md", ".prawduct/changes/records-pass-final-bars.md", "tools/measure-record-findings.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "The branch review set grows without a lifecycle and counts blockers a spanning review cleared as open forever", "recommendation": "Class. Why it broke: since #334, every reader of `coverage.branch_review_ids` treats 'no resolution fact' as 'open', but the gate also clears a superseded blocker by a spanning review, which writes no resolution fact. Members: `critic_consolidate.carried_blocking`, which feeds the verify roll-call directive, the consolidation `NOT DONE` arm and the cache record; `dispositions._summarize` `open_blocking`, through bare `render-dispositions`; and `briefing._branch_findings_line`. Take a branch where a spanning `cumulative` routed around an earlier round's blocker. `check-cumulative-critic` passes. The handoff still says '1 open blocking', and every later `verify-resolutions` consolidates `NOT DONE` and hands its reviewer a roll-call demanding a verdict on a finding whose code may since have moved. That is a round-buying loop of the kind the review-wall-clock P0 exists to stop. The worktree arm makes it unbounded. When the span is empty (the trunk shape, or a branch with no commits yet), the set is every review fact this worktree ever recorded, across all scopes and all time. The old `_rounds_recorded_in_this_worktree` was scoped, and only the round budget narrows by scope now. So on a trunk-based product the census, the roll-call and the handoff line accumulate history monotonically. Fix through the one owner: have the set's consumers ask the gate's own question, a blocker on a review the coverage path no longer passes through is not owed. Alternatively, return only reviews on the current coverage path. Also bound the worktree arm, for example by scope or by the last clean boundary review. Pin both cases: a spanning-cleared blocker is not carried, and a trunk-shape worktree with an old unresolved blocker in another scope does not carry it.", "files": ["plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/dispositions.py", "plugin/lib/briefing.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "record-lint governed-by-gap: .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-backlog-roi-2026-11-18.md:12", "recommendation": "architecture carries 8 `## Direction` norm(s) but this plan disposes of 4 \u2014 each norm needs a recorded disposition, and \"inapplicable, because \u2014\" is one", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-backlog-roi-2026-11-18.md"], "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "record-lint governed-by-gap: .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-backlog-roi-2026-11-18.md:18", "recommendation": "data-model carries 8 `## Direction` norm(s) but this plan disposes of 3 \u2014 each norm needs a recorded disposition, and \"inapplicable, because \u2014\" is one", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-backlog-roi-2026-11-18.md"], "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "record-lint governed-by-gap: .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-backlog-roi-2026-11-18.md:23", "recommendation": "api-contract carries 3 `## Direction` norm(s) but this plan disposes of 2 \u2014 each norm needs a recorded disposition, and \"inapplicable, because \u2014\" is one", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-backlog-roi-2026-11-18.md"], "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-9"}], "counts": {"blocking": 3, "warning": 6, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Two governed_by disposition rationales describe work the bundle did not do; the conformance verdicts themselves hold", "recommendation": "Governing-Artifact Reconciliation. The architecture dispositions credit chunk 06 with 'makes decode loss visible as a warning', but chunk 06 is in `dropped:`. They also say chunk 08 'reuses the existing cmd_stop egress through one extracted helper'. In fact chunk 08 added `git_hygiene.any_pr_for` on the existing `_gh_json` site, behind `worktrees --prs`, and recorded that departure in 843-design.md, the change fragment and the egress_boundary entry. Both 'conforms' verdicts are true of the diff: the new network call is opt-in behind a flag on an existing egress site, and the probe is offline. Every other disposition was checked against the diff and holds, including the data-model departure for #1199, which is recorded as a vetoable DECISION. No departure is unrecorded. The stale rationale clauses are the Records Pass's to weigh.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-backlog-roi-2026-11-18.md"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Chunk 07's written-moment test credits this session for files its own later git operations rewrote", "recommendation": "Inside a tool call, `_written_while_acting` reads the checkout file's mtime and ctime. A checkout, rebase, stash pop or reset that this session runs later rewrites the files of another session's idle commit and stamps them inside that call. That commit is then kept in the span, though #1071 would have dropped it. The docstring names only 'written again since by someone else'. This errs toward counting, the safe direction for the gates. Consider naming the case in the docstring, or not crediting a write whose moment follows the commit's own time.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "'Only a spanning review clears a superseded blocker' survives the #334 sweep in two descriptions", "recommendation": "Chunk 11 says the superseded-blocker prose follows #334, but two sites still say the pre-#334 thing. coverage_algebra.coverage_verdict's docstring (~line 704) says `superseded` 'means no verify pass will revisit it and only a spanning review can clear it'. The comment above next_action's carried arm (critic_consolidate.py ~line 1059) says a carried blocker is 'orphaned onto a superseded round and clearable only by a spanning `cumulative`'. Since #334, an in-set superseded blocker is named by the roll-call. Make both relational (superseded = not the anchor; whether a pass can still clear it is the branch set's question, gates.blocking_remedy_lines), or delete the clause. Prose ceiling: no gate reads either one. Do it only alongside the blocking fix above, which touches both sites anyway.", "files": ["plugin/lib/coverage_algebra.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "building.md's gate/review concurrency paragraph states its exceptions without saying what to do", "recommendation": "Framework Check 8 (Instruction Clarity). 'Two orderings stand: a suite writing a file git does not ignore moves the reviewed tree, so consolidation refuses; `verify-resolutions` refuses mid-run, so it follows the record.' The first case gives a consequence, not an instruction, and the builder cannot tell from it whether their suite writes such a file. Phrase both as imperatives, e.g. 'If the suite writes files git does not ignore, record first, then dispatch; dispatch `verify-resolutions` only after the record finishes.'", "files": ["plugin/methodology/building.md"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Late-requirement capture adds Builder and Critic legs that the Requirements clarity registry row and no artifact template mention", "recommendation": "Framework Check 10 (Pipeline Coverage). building.md now prescribes a `## Late requirements` record shape, and record_lint gained `late-requirement-record`. The 'Requirements clarity' row in cross-cutting-concerns.md still lists only the mid-build tripwires for Builder and the Goal 2/5 checks for Critic. No requirements-artifact template carries the section, so an onboarded product meets it only through building.md. The registry is oracle, so the Records Pass decides whether this is a finding there. Noted here because the coverage change is in subject files.", "files": ["plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/lib/record_lint.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "active_build_plan set to this plan beneath a comment recording that null is the deliberate state", "recommendation": "Records Pass, below the bars. Plan-birth commit e3d9e217 set the deprecated scalar in .prawduct/project-state.yaml, giving no reason. The comment directly above still says 'CLEARED 2026-08-13, and the null is the state rather than an oversight', and planning.md calls the scalar deprecated. Because the plan claims its branch, the pointer binds nothing while the plan is live. Once the release archives the plan, though, the pointer dangles on develop, which this plan does not bind. From then, `briefing` prints '\u26a0 active_build_plan points at a MISSING file' at every develop session start, and `archive-plan` prints its NOTE. Nothing is wrong today. Nulling it before merge avoids that path.", "files": [".prawduct/project-state.yaml"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "A failed other-session-commit record surfaces only on log-only stderr, and a missing .session-start collapses its dedupe key", "recommendation": "`gates.record_dropped_commits` returns its 'was not recorded' notes as `_LogOnlyAdvisory`. At a clean Stop they reach only stderr at exit 0, which hook-surface.md's rule 'A message from a hook that exits 0 ... must use the host's delivery channel or a file the reader opens; plain stderr is logged, read by nobody' warns against. `stats` therefore undercounts silently. Separately, `_read_session_start(...) or \"unknown\"` makes the dedupe key `unknown:<sha>` for every session without the stamp, so a second session's drop of the same commit is deduped away. Both are low stakes because the record is advice. Note them where the observation's yield gets read.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "oid": "O-7"}], "duration_seconds": 306, "scope": "backlog-roi-2026-11-18", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01", "11"], "chunks_proposed": ["01", "11"], "base_reviewed": "cda8c2f9e0a746a3dc726aba9c00591e4fba5665", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-backlog-roi-2026-11-18.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/blind-verification-join.md", ".prawduct/changes/branch-review-set.md", ".prawduct/changes/chunk-credit-merged-verified.md", ".prawduct/changes/closes-cache-miss.md", ".prawduct/changes/control-check-field.md", ".prawduct/changes/detached-refresh-outcome.md", ".prawduct/changes/exit1-covering-mode.md", ".prawduct/changes/gate-review-concurrency.md", ".prawduct/changes/gate-span-and-coverage-slips.md", ".prawduct/changes/late-requirement-capture.md", ".prawduct/changes/measure-record-findings.md", ".prawduct/changes/pushed-branch-never-prd.md", ".prawduct/changes/records-pass-final-bars.md", ".prawduct/changes/round-tally-at-next-action.md", ".prawduct/changes/stop-span-bash-authorship.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md", ".prawduct/project-state.yaml", ".prawduct/research/native-review-paired/README.md", "documentation/issues/1141-requirements.md", "documentation/issues/843-design.md", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/method